    "after_contains": "Identity has to survive these transitions.",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/removal-fence.svg\" alt=\"A removal timeline shows CPU object removal, cessation of new render references, continued GPU buffer occupancy, a completion fence, and later slot reuse with an incremented generation.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"600\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>In this schematic removal of the room door’s indicator, the blue allocation remains occupied after the red removal line because previously submitted GPU work may still read it. The fence, rather than the gameplay deletion time, permits physical reuse. Reusing slot 42 with generation 8 prevents an old logical handle for generation 7 from naming the new occupant. This is an illustrative lifetime contract, not a claim that Unreal uses this exact handle encoding.</figcaption></figure>"
