    "chapter": "the-courtyard-needs-more-than-static-meshes",
    "after_contains": "A ray marcher approximates this integral",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/alpha-and-volume.svg\" alt=\"Red and blue half-transparent panes are composited in both orders over white, yielding different overlap colors. A computed fog transmittance curve falls from one toward zero as distance increases.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"650\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>For a room display-case extension, read the overlap patches: red over blue gives (0.75,0.25,0.50), while swapping their order gives (0.50,0.25,0.75), using the shown unit-RGB compositing arithmetic over white. A pane is a surface event with an order. A humidifier’s mist instead attenuates continuously along distance: the plotted curve is T(d)=exp(−0.5d). Full volume rendering additionally integrates light scattered and emitted into the ray. Displayed colors illustrate the arithmetic; this is not a complete linear-light display-management example.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
  {
    "chapter": "the-world-is-not-one-tree",
    "after_contains": "For the indoor door, imagine an actor containing",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/gate-attachment.svg\" alt=\"A top-down room door swings from zero to ninety degrees about a hinge at −150,200 centimeters. Its indicator is marked at a sixty-centimeter local offset before and after rotation.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"620\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The brown door becomes the green door while the hinge stays fixed. Both yellow indicator positions are computed from the same parent rotation applied to the indicator’s 60-centimeter local offset. The native room setup attaches DoorPanel and AttachedIndicator as siblings beneath TeachingRoot. Both inherit the hinge transform; neither requires the other as a parent. The emissive indicator mesh has its own material and no LightComponent.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
  {
    "chapter": "motion-is-more-than-a-matrix",
    "after_contains": "Skinning introduces transforms inside the object.",
    "html": "<figure><img src=\"assets/walkthrough/weighted-skinning.svg\" alt=\"Three computed triangulated sleeve strips show rest, previous, and current deformation. The tracked red vertex moves from 2,0 to 2,0.5 to 2,1 under the displayed two-bone weighted calculation.\" width=\"1040\" height=\"670\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>This computed sleeve closeup isolates the arithmetic used by the room’s captured 68-bone <code>SK_Mannequin</code>, posed by <code>ThirdPersonWalk</code>. Its invented two-bone weights do not reproduce the asset’s stored bone influences or its captured poses at 0.10 s and 0.45 s. The red vertex is the prose’s 0.75/0.25 example. Its two current transformed positions are (2,0) and (2,4), so the result is (2,1); the previous pose instead used (2,2) for the second result, producing (2,0.5). The strip is actually computed with weights varying along its length. This is an illustrative software deformation using simple bone translations, not an Unreal skeletal-mesh capture. Its retained previous result is the information motion reconstruction needs.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
